# Foreign git hooks land here. Ignore them; never commit them; never delete them.
#
# Workshop points every consumer's `core.hooksPath` at this directory
# (Tools/sync-skill-symlinks.sh, docs/templates/cloud-sessions/agent-session-start.sh)
# so check-skill-roster-freshness.sh can run as post-checkout/post-merge/post-rewrite.
# `core.hooksPath` is exclusive: once it is set, git ignores .git/hooks/ entirely,
# so ANY other hook installer — `git lfs install`, `pre-commit install`, husky, a
# project's own bootstrap — must write into this tracked directory for its hook to
# run at all. Foreign hooks are therefore expected here, and this is not specific
# to LFS.
#
# Two consequences, both counter-intuitive:
#
#   - Never commit one. It is a consumer's local installation, and publishing it
#     would run it in every other consumer — including repos where the tool it
#     belongs to is not installed, whose not-found branch typically exits non-zero
#     and blocks the operation.
#
#   - Never delete one as cleanup. Deleting does not restore a default: with
#     `core.hooksPath` redirected here, the stray file IS the active hook, so
#     removing it silently disables the installing tool's check (LFS push
#     validation, for example) in the repo that installed it.
#
# Adding a hook to Workshop itself means adding it below AND to the expected
# manifest in tests/test-hooks-directory-manifest.sh, which asserts that the
# tracked contents of this directory are exactly this set.

*
!.gitignore
!post-checkout
!post-merge
!post-rewrite
